## El Cajon Lemon Law** and Used Vehicles

California lemon law rights extend to used vehicles. Here’s how it works:

## Dealer Warranties

Most used vehicles sold at dealerships in California come with dealer warranties. These are generally valid for 30 days after purchase or before 1,000 miles accrue on the odometer. If your used vehicle meets the qualifications of California lemon law within the terms of the dealer warranty, it can be processed in the same way as a new vehicle, with the help of a California lemon law attorney.

Your Rights

If your used vehicle was sold with a dealer warranty, the dealership is required to manage the repair process or provide a buyback if the vehicle meets California lemon law’s qualifications.

“As Is” Agreements

“As is” agreements mean the consumer takes full responsibility for the condition of the vehicle upon purchase - and there are no California lemon law protections in place. These agreements should be visible in the buyer’s guide.

If there is no specification of a dealer warranty or “as is” agreement, you may be eligible for benefits via an [implied warranty](https://codes.findlaw.com/ca/civil-code/civ-sect-1791-1/). Implied warranties are tricky - and you’ll need a skilled California lemon law attorney to fight for fair compensation.

